/* std/thread: the thread surface. Stage A2 of MK9-S3-M1 added the fence, and the
   threads milestone MK6-S1-M1 adds `spawn` and `join` to this module (its stage
   C1 in plan/mk9-memory-model.md).

       fun fence(order: Ordering)

   The fence, and the module's surface is documented here rather than declared,
   exactly as `std/atomic` documents the atomic methods it declares nowhere: a
   fence cannot be written in Kelp, so the compiler lowers it, and the parameter
   type is `std/atomic`'s `Ordering`, which a declaration would have to name
   across a C boundary that repeats no Kelp `enum`.

   The fence has no cell and no receiver. `SeqCst` means one total order over
   every sequentially consistent operation, and a fence is the one operation
   that joins that order while naming no location, which is why it is a free
   function rather than an `Atomic<T>.fence` (MK9 open question 9.2, resolved
   this way by A2). It lives in `std/thread` rather than beside the atomic
   builtin because that is where the thread surface goes, so C1 adds `spawn` and
   `join` to a module instead of inventing one.

   A program pays the same `impl "std/atomic"` line every atomic call site pays,
   plus the module qualifier, because that is where `Ordering` is declared:
   `thread.fence(order = atomic.Ordering.SeqCst)`. The qualifier is the enum's,
   not this module's, so nothing here imports it.

   v1 has exactly one ordering, `SeqCst` (MK9 section 3), so `order` has exactly
   one legal value. The argument is the slot that reserves the position for
   `Relaxed`/`Acquire`/`Release`: naming one of those is the ordinary
   unknown-variant error until the variant exists, and adding it widens the enum
   instead of changing this signature.

   A fence is one instruction and changes nothing a single-threaded program can
   observe: `__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)` on the C side and
   `fence seq_cst` on the LLVM side. */
